WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.633
Pretraining.

2
00:00:01.933 --> 00:00:05.498
Everything a language model knows comes from one simple game.

3
00:00:05.498 --> 00:00:06.667
Here's how it works.

4
00:00:07.867 --> 00:00:11.533
Pretraining is the huge first phase that creates a base model.

5
00:00:11.833 --> 00:00:13.621
No labels, no instructions.

6
00:00:13.621 --> 00:00:16.667
Just text, and one task: guess the next token.

7
00:00:16.967 --> 00:00:22.233
Orbital mechanics, Python syntax, all of it is a side effect of getting good at that game.

8
00:00:23.433 --> 00:00:27.000
Here is the loop, repeated across most of the readable internet.

9
00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:29.100
Show the network a slice of text.

10
00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:30.900
It predicts the next token.

11
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:34.400
We compare that guess with the token that really came next.

12
00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:38.067
Then nudge the weights slightly, and repeat trillions of times.

13
00:00:39.267 --> 00:00:40.933
The result is a base model.

14
00:00:41.233 --> 00:00:43.700
It continues your text instead of answering it.

15
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:48.167
Ask a question and it may reply with more questions, like a quiz page.

16
00:00:48.467 --> 00:00:51.800
Instruct models are tuned further to actually reply.

17
00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:54.267
Same start, very different behaviour.

18
00:00:55.467 --> 00:00:56.967
Here's the difference in code.

19
00:00:57.267 --> 00:00:59.667
We load a base model and an instruct model.

20
00:00:59.967 --> 00:01:01.700
Then ask both the same question.

21
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:03.693
The base model rambles on.

22
00:01:03.693 --> 00:01:07.533
The instruct model answers: minus one eighty three Celsius.

23
00:01:08.733 --> 00:01:10.600
So what does this mean for your work?

24
00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:13.700
Weights give you language skill and general physics.

25
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:17.800
Context gives you the facts, because weights stop learning at the cutoff.

26
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:21.300
Take Mission Control question answering.

27
00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:24.900
Its logs were written last week, long after any cutoff.

28
00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:26.900
The weights provide language skill.

29
00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:28.833
Retrieval supplies the facts.

30
00:01:30.033 --> 00:01:30.802
Let's recap.

31
00:01:30.802 --> 00:01:34.067
Pretraining is next token prediction at huge scale.

32
00:01:34.367 --> 00:01:36.900
It produces a base model that completes text.

33
00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:40.600
Knowledge has a cutoff, so fresh facts come from retrieval.

34
00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:43.829
Weights for competence, context for knowledge.

35
00:01:43.829 --> 00:01:45.167
Next up: fine-tuning.
